Problem 6 (Life Sciences and Polymers section, problem 3). [Stages of pyruvate metabolism]

One of the most important metabolic pathways of pyruvate ($\mathbf{I}$) transformation was first described in a simplified form and did not include one of the stages known today. When disclosing the pathway, the researchers were aware of experimental facts (a – f) given below. In this task you will have to ascertain the sequence of stages of pyruvate metabolism.

A number of anions of di- and tricarboxylic acids are involved in the pathway. Formulae and names of the anions are given hereunder in a random order.

https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Eisatopon/chemistry-olympiads-bank/main/images/imcho-2014-2-6-fig1.png

Experimental studies showed that:

a) all stages are catalyzed by corresponding enzymes;

b) transformation of succinate ($\mathbf{IV}$) is catalyzed by succinate dehydrogenase. The enzyme is inhibited by malonate ($\mathbf{X}$), which results in accumulation of metabolites $\mathbf{IV}$, $\mathbf{V}$, and $\mathbf{VIII}$;

c) only reactions of two-electron oxidation (in some cases accompanied by decarboxylation), hydration and dehydration are met along the metabolic pathway;

d) oxidation reactions require either co-enzyme $\ce{NAD+}$/NADH or co-factor FAD/$\ce{FADH2}$ (oxidized/reduced forms, respectively);

e) incubation of $\mathbf{I}$ and $\mathbf{II}$ in the presence of the enzyme leads to $\mathbf{VIII}$;

f) addition of even small amounts of any of the metabolites $\mathbf{II}$ – $\mathbf{IX}$ results in an abrupt acceleration of pyruvate consumption.

1. Write down the sequence of all stages of pyruvate metabolism.

2. Indicate the stages involving oxidation of an organic substrate.

3. In what form do the co-enzyme and co-factor enter the reactions mentioned in i. 2?

4. Which of the compounds $\mathbf{I}$ – $\mathbf{X}$ is(are) optically active? Draw the $R$-isomer(s) of this(ese) compound(s).

Another metabolic pathway (in addition to the described above) occurs in some organisms at the stage of their rapid growth. Formation of one equivalent of succinate from two equivalents of pyruvate turns out to be the overall result of the latter pathway.

5. Write down the sequence of all stages of the additional metabolic pathway if:

g) it involves compounds $\mathbf{I}$, $\mathbf{II}$, $\mathbf{III}$, $\mathbf{IV}$, $\mathbf{VI}$, $\mathbf{VIII}$, $\mathbf{IX}$, as well as the compound $\mathbf{A}$, which is not utilized in the main metabolic pathway;

h) two pyruvate molecules enter the pathway at different points;

i) succinate is produced from iso-citrate;

j) four stages in both pathways coincide.

Problem 4. [Iodide oxidation by hydrogen peroxide: lag period with ascorbic acid and molybdate catalysis]

Oxidation of iodide ion with hydrogen peroxide in acidic medium occurs slowly. When we add a compound instantly binding molecular iodine, the iodine color does not appear for a certain time (lag period). When $0.125\,\mathrm{M}$ $\ce{H2O2}$ and $0.05\,\mathrm{M}$ KI (concentrations are given in the reaction mixture at the start of the reaction) and ascorbic acid (AA, see Fig.; its concentrations at the start of the reaction were $1.25\cdot10^{-4}$, $5\cdot10^{-4}$ and $1.25\cdot10^{-3}\,\mathrm{M}$) were mixed at pH 4.5, the iodine color appeared after $\tau = 4$, 16 and 40 s, respectively.

https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Eisatopon/chemistry-olympiads-bank/main/images/imcho-2017-1-4-fig1.png

1. Write down:

a) Slow reaction (1) of oxidation of iodide ion with hydrogen peroxide in acidic medium;

b) Fast reaction (2) that retards the appearance of molecular iodine color (denote dehydroascorbic acid as $\ce{C6H6O6}$);

c) Equation relating the time of appearance of color $\tau$ with the concentration of ascorbic acid in solution (calculate the numerical value of the coefficient of proportionality);

d) Kinetic equation of the rate of consumption of $\ce{H2O2}$ according to reaction (1) at a constant pH, taking into account that it has the first order in iodide ion (denote the rate constant as $k_1$).

2. Given that the rates of consumption of AA and reaction (1) are equal, calculate:

a) The rate of consumption of AA by reaction (2) in $\mathrm{mol/(L\cdot s)}$;

b) The value of the rate constant $k_1$ of reaction (1), taking the current concentrations of the components equal to the original ones;

c) The duration of the lag period $\tau$ for $0.05\,\mathrm{M}$ $\ce{H2O2}$, $0.05\,\mathrm{M}$ KI and $1.25\cdot10^{-3}\,\mathrm{M}$ AA at pH 4.5, assuming current concentrations of the components are equal to their initial concentrations.

3. Molybdate ion catalyzes the oxidation of iodide with peroxide, since supramolybdic acid $\ce{H2MoO5}$ and its anions oxidize iodide faster than $\ce{H2O2}$ does.

a) Assuming that molybdate is present only in the form of $\ce{H2MoO5}$, write down the kinetic equation for this reaction in its general form (denote the rate constant as $k_\mathrm{Mo}$).

b) Estimate the value of the constant $k_\mathrm{Mo}$ in presence of $1\cdot10^{-4}\,\mathrm{M}$ $\ce{MoO4^{2-}}$, if the lag period was 50 s for the conditions specified in point 2c.

c) Calculate the duration of the lag period for $3\cdot10^{-4}\,\mathrm{M}$ molybdate.

Problem 3 (Life Sciences and Polymers section, problem II-3). [Steroidogenesis]

Steroidogenesis is the process of the biosynthesis of steroid hormones from cholesterol. To study this process, several steroids shown below were isolated from the human adrenal cortex:

https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Eisatopon/chemistry-olympiads-bank/main/images/imcho-2025-2-3-fig1.png

It is known about steroidogenesis in the human body that:

• all the metabolites isolated from the adrenal gland (and no others) are involved in it;
• it counts for 21 redox enzymatic reactions (12 of which involve molecular oxygen) and one hydration reaction;
• it involves co-enzymes $\ce{NAD+}$ (which acts as the main electron acceptor, being reduced to NADH) and NADPH (which acts as the main electron donor, being oxidized to $\ce{NADP+}$);
• cholesterol and 21-hydroxysteroids are substrates in only one enzymatic reaction each;
• the metabolite $\mathbf{E}$ is formed from cholesterol in three steps.

II-3.1 (1.75 pt) Which of the steroids considered in the task cannot be quantitatively isolated from the homogenate (finely chopped adrenal tissue) by extraction with a nonpolar solvent:

a) after treatment of the homogenate with an aqueous alkali solution;

b) one hour after treatment of the homogenate with a solution of silver oxide in aqueous ammonia?

II-3.2 (1 pt) Some metabolites are the products of two simultaneously (but no more than two) different reactions. Calculate the number ($N$) of such metabolites.

II-3.3 (11 pt) In the Answer Sheets, write down all a) redox and b) hydrolytic reactions of steroidogenesis by filling in the blanks in the schemes. Be sure to include all the compounds participating in each reaction by providing the corresponding stoichiometric coefficients.

II-3.4 (1.25 pt) Reconstruct the scheme of steroidogenesis by combining all the reactions recorded in the previous question into chains of sequential transformations (compounds other than $\mathbf{A}$–$\mathbf{R}$ can be omitted).

Problem 12 (Inorganic Chemistry section, problem II-12). [Chalcogenides of element X]

The element $\mathbf{X}$ exhibits many allotropes; it forms one of the hardest and the most refractory elementary substances. Its chemistry is extremely complex and interesting from both practical and theoretical perspectives.

Hydrolysis-unstable needles $\mathbf{A}$ were obtained by fast heating of a mixture of $\mathbf{X}$ sulfide and $\ce{S8}$ (reaction 1). Although the substance is poorly studied and has found no practical application yet, it possesses a very interesting high-symmetry structure, somewhat like that of porphyrin. It contains no $\mathbf{X}{-}\mathbf{X}$ bonds and no $-\mathrm{S}_3-$ or $-\mathrm{S}_4-$ fragments. The unit cell parameters of $\mathbf{A}$ are: $a = 12.158\,\text{Å}$, $b = 4.089\,\text{Å}$, $c = 21.961\,\text{Å}$; angle $= 107.65^\circ$, density $\rho = 1.9109\,\mathrm{g/cm^3}$, number of formula units $Z = 2$.

https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Eisatopon/chemistry-olympiads-bank/main/images/imcho-2025-2-12-fig1.png

II-12.1 (2.25 pt) a) Calculate the molar mass $M$ (g/mol) of $\mathbf{A}$;
b) decipher $\mathbf{X}$ and $\mathbf{A}$;
c) draw structure of $\mathbf{A}$;
d) write the equation of the reaction 1.

The compound $\mathbf{B}$ is formed upon heating of stoichiometric amounts of $\ce{Rb2S}$, $\ce{S8}$, and $\mathbf{X}$ at $600\,^\circ\mathrm{C}$ (reaction 2). $\mathbf{B}$ consists of five-membered rings that are linked via common $\mathbf{X}$ atoms into polymer chains. The compound $\mathbf{C}$ is obtained in the case of heating of a mixture of $\ce{Tl2S}$, $\ce{S8}$, and $\mathbf{X}$ at $850\,^\circ\mathrm{C}$ (reaction 3). The structure of $\mathbf{C}$ is almost analogous to that of $\mathbf{B}$: only an additional sulfur atom is incorporated in every third ring. There are no $\mathbf{X}{-}\mathbf{X}$ bonds in $\mathbf{B}$.

II-12.2 (2 pt) a) Determine $\mathbf{B}$ and $\mathbf{C}$;
b) draw structure of the anion in $\mathbf{B}$;
c) write equation of the reaction 2;
d) write equation of the reaction 3.

New compounds with anions containing the elements $\mathbf{X}$ and $\mathbf{Y}$ are intensively synthesized and investigated now, forming a rapidly developed branch of chemistry.

The compound $\mathbf{D}$ with the composition $\mathrm{Li}_a\mathbf{X}_b\mathbf{Y}_c$ (where $a$, $b$, and $c$ are natural numbers, they are consecutive terms of an arithmetic progression) is a well-known lithium-ion conductor, which can be used as electrolyte in lithium-ion batteries. This substance is obtained by heating a mixture of the binary compounds $\mathbf{E}$ and $\mathbf{F}$ to $750\,^\circ\mathrm{C}$ (reaction 4). Since $\mathbf{D}$ is the sole product in this reaction and is easily oxidized, its synthesis must be carried out under oxygen-free conditions. $\mathbf{F}$ hydrolyzes readily with the evolution of a gas.

Other compounds related to $\mathbf{D}$ are also actively studied due to their high mechanical strength and tunable transport properties, to be used as insulators, metallic conductors, or superconductors. The derivatives containing the element $\mathbf{Z}$ with the composition $\mathbf{Z}_m\mathbf{X}_n\mathbf{Y}_k$ (where $m$, $n$, and $k$ are natural numbers) are of particular interest.

$\mathbf{Z}$ is a soft, silvery-white metal that exhibits two (besides 0) oxidation states in its compounds. $\mathbf{G}$ is often used at the first step of the synthesis of derivatives $\mathbf{Z}_m\mathbf{X}_n\mathbf{Y}_k$. $\mathbf{G}$ has a NaCl-type structure. Like $\mathbf{F}$, $\mathbf{G}$ hydrolyzes easily with the evolution of a gas.

https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Eisatopon/chemistry-olympiads-bank/main/images/imcho-2025-2-12-fig2.png

The compound $\mathbf{H}$ was obtained upon heating a mixture of $\mathbf{E}$ and $\mathbf{G}$ at $1273\,^\circ\mathrm{C}$ (reaction 5). In practice, $\mathbf{H}$ is used primarily as a precursor for obtaining other derivatives $\mathbf{Z}_m\mathbf{X}_n\mathbf{Y}_k$. For example, $\mathbf{H}$ is oxidized by bromine at $1073\,^\circ\mathrm{C}$ to form the compound $\mathbf{I}$ (reaction 6). The coefficient $m$ is the same in the formulae of $\mathbf{H}$ and $\mathbf{I}$, but they contain different anions. The structure of the anion in $\mathbf{I}$ is shown in the figure.

An attempt was made to obtain a non-stoichiometric compound $\mathbf{J}$ by reacting $\mathbf{H}$ with $\mathbf{F}$, but the synthesis did not yield the expected result. $\mathbf{J}$ can be prepared theoretically by intercalation (insertion) of lithium into $\mathbf{I}$ by passing a $20.492\,\mathrm{mAh}$ of charge per $1000\,\mathrm{mg}$ of the final product. Ultimately, $\mathbf{J}$ was obtained by heating a mixture of $\mathbf{D}$ and the higher chloride of $\mathbf{Z}$ at $850\,^\circ\mathrm{C}$ (reaction 7).

The anion of $\mathbf{D}$ is linear; the product $A_\mathrm{r}(\mathbf{Y})\cdot A_\mathrm{r}(\mathbf{X})$ is slightly lower than $A_\mathrm{r}(\mathbf{Z})$, and the anions of $\mathbf{H}$ and $\mathbf{I}$ have the same simplest formula.

II-12.3 (8.25 pt) a) Calculate the molar mass $M$ (g/mol) of $\mathbf{D}$;
b) decipher the elements $\mathbf{Y}$ and $\mathbf{Z}$, and the compounds $\mathbf{D}$–$\mathbf{F}$;
c) calculate the molar mass $M$ (g/mol) of $\mathbf{J}$
d) decipher $\mathbf{G}$–$\mathbf{J}$.

Reactions 5 and 7 are accompanied by the evolution of the same gas; $\mathbf{E}$ is formed in reaction 7.

II-12.4 (1.5 pt) Write the equations of the reactions 4–7.

II-12.5 (1 pt) a) Calculate the capacity ($C$, mAh/g) of $\ce{LiC6}$;
b) compare capacities of $\ce{LiC6}$ and $\mathbf{J}$ (choose among the options below).

1) $C(\ce{LiC6}) > C(\mathbf{J})$; 2) $C(\ce{LiC6}) < C(\mathbf{J})$; 3) $C(\ce{LiC6}) = C(\mathbf{J})$; 4) Insufficient data
